  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nEarlier showers have subsided this afternoon with the gradual exit \nof a surface cyclone off to the east, with high pressure building in \nfrom the west. The vertical profile of this surface cyclone has \nremained stacked through the morning and early afternoon, with \nmarginal dCVA shifting the primary upper-level trough off towards \nthe east at a snail's pace. As a result, cloud cover has persisted \nover much of central/eastern MO, with sustained north/northwesterly \nadvection providing cooler and drier air to the forecast area. This \nsynoptic setup is helping to dampen temperatures for today across \ncentral/eastern MO, where cloud cover is preventing substantial \nsurface heating through the day. However, for areas further \nnorth/west where the sun has emerged through the clouds, \ntemperatures have risen more near the 80s. This synoptic setup has \nalso prompted a strong pressure gradient to develop between the \nslowly retreating upper-level trough and approaching upper-level \nridge. Gusts of 35-40 mph have been observed across the area as low-\nlevel winds mix down to the surface alongside ambient winds around \n15-20 mph, keeping the day pleasant albeit a little windy.\n\nAs the surface cyclone and upper-level trough continue their slow \nretreat off towards the east, the aforementioned upper-level ridge \nwill continue pushing into the central CONUS from the west. As this \noccurs, skies will clear out and winds will slow under a lessening \npressure gradient force. There is some potential for fog development \nacross northern MO tonight with these conditions setting up, \nespecially for areas that saw rainfall earlier today. However, with \nsustained winds still remaining higher overnight, this potential \nwould remain patchy at best. The remainder of the weekend will \nremain pleasant with continued advection of cooler, drier air under \na northerly wind pattern, before surface winds shift out of the \nsouth by the beginning of the week. The forecast remains dry through \nearly midweek, with temperatures slowly climbing into the mid/high \n80s across much of the forecast area. \n\nBy Tuesday, the upper-level ridge will continue getting pushed \noff towards the east by shortwave disturbances at the base of an\nupper-level trough near the Hudson Bay, placing the forecast \narea under quasi-zonal flow. Under this flow pattern, PoPs \nincrease by midweek from several low-level shortwaves traversing\nthe flow between the upper-level trough to the north and a \nbuilding ridge over the southwestern CONUS. Deterministic and \nprobabilistic guidance continue highlighting northern MO for the\ngreatest potential for precipitation during this period \n(40-60%) by Wednesday evening into Thursday morning. However, \nsome finer details of the forecast still remain uncertain at \nthis time, making it difficult to ascertain exact hazards. With \nthis consistent signal, future forecasts will need to monitor \nthis system for greater confidence in evolving threats. Beyond \nthis, greater deviation in synoptic guidance continues to \ndecrease confidence in the evolution of the forecast through the\nend of next week regarding future PoPs. Under consistent \nsoutherly flow in the lowest levels of the atmosphere, \ntemperatures by the end of the forecast look to rise into the \nhigh 80s and low 90s.",
